The '''Levante''' ({{IPA|bl|leˈβante|lang}}; {{Langx|mxi|لِيبَانْتِي|Libánti}}; {{Literal translation|the place of rising|the east}}), also known as the '''Aethiopic Coast''', is a name used to refer to the territory of Balisca that lies east of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], on than the [[Abyala|Abyalan]] [[Aethiopian Ocean|Aethiopian]] coast. It roughly corresponds to the former area under the influence of the [[Hesperian League]], but has no modern geopolitical definition. Rather, it broadly includes the communities of [[Hesperia]] (all regions except [[Trás-as-Montanyas]]), Aethiopic [[Araucaria]] ([[Luzamontaña]], [[Juyú]] and [[Brasal]]), the [[Castejón Metropolis]] and all regions of [[Cerdanya]] east of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] (and also excluding the insular [[Cap de Pingüins]] region).

Among inhabitants of the Levante, the term is rarely used. Its literal meaning is "the east", and thus makes sense only from the perspective of those who live to the west of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]].

== Toponym and composition ==
The term "Levante" derives from the Sabir word for "east" or "the place of rising," referring to the sunrise over the Aethiopic Ocean as seen from the western slopes of the Matarrañya. The name entered common usage among [[Volisania|Volisanian]] colonists on the [[Salsipuedes Sea|Salsipuedes]] side of the range, who referred to the territories beyond the mountains as ''Llevant''—the east. The Mozbayani form ''Libánti'' (لِيبَانْتِي) is a direct borrowing from Sabir, reflecting the Bayani linguistic influence that pervades the Aethiopic coastal toponymy.

The geographic scope of the Levante has never been precisely defined. In its broadest usage, it encompasses all Baliscan territory east of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] watershed—from the [[Sea of Shuainia]] in the north to the subtropical coast of [[Jaguarundi]] in the south. In its narrowest usage, it refers only to the urbanized coastal corridor between [[Porciúncula]] and [[Concepción]], excluding the more sparsely populated northern and southern extremities. The [[Confederal Institute of Statistics|Confederal Statistical Institute]] uses an intermediate definition that includes the four communities of [[Araucaria]], [[Castejón Metropolis]], [[Hesperia]] (excluding Trás-as-Montanyas) and [[Dríadas]] (in [[Cerdanya]]), yielding a population of approximately 133.7 million.

The composition of the Levante reflects the historical fragmentation of the Aethiopic coast among competing colonial and post-colonial polities. The [[Hesperian League]], which dominated the coast from the 16th through the mid 18th centuries, bequeathed a shared commercial and maritime culture that persists despite the region's subsequent political reorganization. The League's legacy is visible in the coastal cities' shared architectural vocabulary—arcaded waterfronts, coral-stone construction, and the distinctive ''torre de guaita'' (watchtower) that marks every historic port—and in the persistence of Sabir-derived maritime terminology across the region's languages.

